manifest=json.loads((ROOT/aux).read_text());assert manifest['schema']=='esp.meaning-model/v1' and manifest['model']['model_id']=='intfloat/e5-base-v2'
notes,authority=load('review_notes.json'),load('authority_map.json');assert notes['findings'][-1]['id']=='Q338' and authority['entries'][-1]['id']=='A913'
findings=[
 {'id':'Q339','title':'BERT drops the tokenizer attention mask before attention and sentence pooling','source':'crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/provider.rs:338;crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/tokenizer.rs:92,118;crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/attention.rs:107;crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/model.rs:130','status':'verified complete current tokenizer/provider/model/attention source; no real or synthetic BERT numerical run','evidence':'BertTokenizer uses BatchLongest right padding and returns real-token/pad attention_mask. forward_sentence_batch consumes input_ids and never passes the mask. BertSelfAttention softmax processes every position and mean_pool averages every position. A short sentence batched with a longer one therefore admits pad embeddings to attention and pooling, despite the sentence-transformers compatibility claim. Both sync and async provider paths share this forward helper. Reference fixtures embed each sentence alone, so they cannot exercise the mixed-length batch case. Shape/finiteness tests do not establish padding invariance.','diagnostics':[trace_name],'repair_triage':{'disposition':'document substantial model-boundary repair; active audit remains read-only','reason':'Thread one mask through model/encoder/layers/attention and masked pooling, then compare the same sentence alone and in mixed-length batches against a numerical reference. Preserve this as a larger high-effect issue rather than spending an hour rebuilding model/GPU infrastructure during full-source reading.'}},
 {'id':'Q340','title':'The batched index accelerator loses the CPU index key tie-break','source':'crates/intel/esp/src/embed/index_burn.rs:82,113,148;crates/intel/esp/src/embed/index.rs:207','status':'verified exact source ordering contracts; Burn/CPU-GPU execution not performed','evidence':'VectorIndex::nearest orders equal scores by K and explicitly promises corpus-determined top-k. nearest_over_index and affinity_pairs_over_index collect the HashMap in arbitrary order, while cosine_top_k stable-sorts only scores and maps retained positions back to those arbitrary keys. They do not even require K:Ord. Equal nonzero vectors or zero-score rows can therefore choose different keys/pairs from the CPU route, contrary to drop-in pair equivalence. The source parity fixture intentionally avoids head ties and cannot prove this boundary. No actual GPU mismatch receipt is claimed.','diagnostics':[trace_name],'repair_triage':{'disposition':'documented under active read-only audit goal','reason':'Give the keyed accelerator the same stable corpus/key ordering or share a deterministic ranking policy. Preserve the row-position API semantics separately.'}},
 {'id':'Q341','title':'Batched cosine uses a different near-zero policy from the CPU vector index','source':'crates/intel/esp/src/embed/index_burn.rs:103;crates/intel/esp/src/embed/index.rs:188','status':'verified formulas in current source; no Burn tensor execution','evidence':'CPU cosine returns zero whenever either vector norm is <=f32::EPSILON. Batched normalization instead divides by norm+1e-12. For finite one-component vectors [1e-8], CPU yields exactly0 while the batched formula normalizes them close to1 and its dot product is close to1. Thus the source claim that degenerate handling matches VectorIndex is false beyond the exact-zero fixture. These are admitted nonzero vectors of valid dimension; ordinary normalized embedding inputs do not establish this edge case.','diagnostics':[trace_name],'repair_triage':{'disposition':'documented under active read-only audit goal','reason':'Share an explicit degenerate-vector threshold and behavior across dense/sparse/Burn paths, with a finite near-zero parity case. Keep the invariant distinct from empirical GPU tolerances.'}},
 {'id':'Q342','title':'SemanticSearch accepts a prebuilt index with the wrong metric and its reads disagree','source':'crates/intel/esp/src/embed/search.rs:92,102,207,238,272','status':'reproduced on freshly compiled unchanged portable core for both dense and sparse stores','evidence':'with_index/with_sparse_index check dimensions only. nearest/search use the supplied index metric; metric() and scores() use the provider metric. A cosine provider paired with a valid two-dimensional Euclidean index is accepted, reports Cosine, ranks [1,1] above [10,0] for query[1,0], while scores() assigns the latter the higher cosine. Dense and sparse fixtures reproduce the same contradiction with valid constructor inputs. Existing matching-dimension tests also happen to match metrics.','diagnostics':['embedding_core_probe_receipt.json'],'repair_triage':{'disposition':'documented under active read-only audit goal','reason':'Check metric agreement during reconstruction or make the authoritative metric an explicit caller decision applied consistently. Matching dimension alone cannot certify vector-space identity.'}},
 {'id':'Q343','title':'Vector deserialization bypasses the invariants enforced by public constructors','source':'crates/intel/esp/src/embed/sparse.rs:38,111;crates/intel/esp/src/embed/index.rs:78,134;crates/intel/esp/src/embed/persistence.rs:234','status':'reproduced fresh serde boundary and actual vector APIs; no live Eidetic store/network corruption run','evidence':'Derived Deserialize constructs SparseVector and VectorIndex private storage directly. Valid JSON with dimensions2 and sparse entry index7 deserializes successfully, then to_dense panics; from_pairs rejects the same entry. A dense index declared dimension2 with a stored one-element vector deserializes and nearest accepts a two-element query, zip-truncating to a plausible score1, while insert rejects that vector. The typed persistence loaders delegate to load_typed and add no vector invariant check. Content hashes/schema identity alone do not establish sorted/in-range/coalesced entries or per-entry dimension.','diagnostics':['embedding_core_probe_receipt.json'],'repair_triage':{'disposition':'documented under active read-only audit goal','reason':'Validate or reconstruct admitted vectors/index entries when decoding. Keep generic representation validation explicit; do not treat an intact hash as proof of internal mathematical invariants.'}},
 {'id':'Q344','title':'The vector rank comparator is not total when accepted scores include NaN','source':'crates/intel/esp/src/embed/index.rs:134,207,226','status':'fresh exact rank_order comparator cycle plus public NaN insertion; no sort panic or nondeterministic product run claimed','evidence':'insert admits non-finite vector components, and rank_order handles unordered scores by key while retaining score order for finite pairs. Exact comparator tuples a=(key1,score1),b=(key2,NaN),c=(key3,score2) give a<b,b<c,c<a. This violates the claimed total order and the sort/partition comparator requirement. The fixture calls the original crate-private helper and separately proves a NaN vector is accepted publicly. It does not claim a particular standard-library sorting outcome.','diagnostics':['embedding_core_probe_receipt.json'],'repair_triage':{'disposition':'documented under active read-only audit goal','reason':'Choose and enforce a finite-score admission rule or a genuine total floating-point order, including scores produced by overflow. Use the same rule in accelerators.'}},
 {'id':'Q345','title':'Affinity can create links with zero requested neighbours and exceed its per-row top-k','source':'crates/intel/esp/src/embed/affinity.rs:93,94,96;crates/intel/esp/src/embed/index_burn.rs:161','status':'reproduced fresh portable affinity; matching accelerator recipe identified by source only','evidence':'Affinity asks nearest(top_k+1), assumes self is in that result, filters self but never takes top_k afterward. Two equal cosine vectors at keys0/1 with top_k0 still create one link because both queries return key0. With dot-product vectors1,2,3 and top_k1, the lowest-magnitude vector selects two other entries since self is outside the head; the final pair set includes all3pairs. The docs allow the index metric, including dot product, and promise per-entry top-k. Batched affinity repeats the same self-in-head assumption; it was not executed here.','diagnostics':['embedding_core_probe_receipt.json'],'repair_triage':{'disposition':'documented under active read-only audit goal','reason':'Exclude self and enforce the requested neighbor count after filtering; define zero as empty or refuse it explicitly. Use checked/saturating arithmetic for the extra candidate rather than leaving usize::MAX as a separate panic edge.'}},
 {'id':'Q346','title':'BERT accepts an activation name but executes GELU regardless of it','source':'crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/config.rs:27;crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/loader.rs:158,395;crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/feed_forward.rs:45','status':'verified config/load/assembly/math source; no non-GELU checkpoint executed','evidence':'BertConfig deserializes hidden_act as an arbitrary String. Artifact and in-memory constructors deserialize it and validate only expected weight dtype/shape. BertIntermediate never reads that field and always applies gelu. A structurally valid checkpoint declaring another activation is neither refused nor executed with its requested math. Supported presets use GELU; their shape tests cannot establish the generic HF-layout loader contract. Public mutable config can also reach panic-based shape checks, which remains a separate admission follow-up.','diagnostics':[trace_name],'repair_triage':{'disposition':'documented under active read-only audit goal','reason':'Validate the supported activation explicitly at config admission, or implement a bounded activation dispatch. Refusal is preferable to silently returning vectors under different math.'}},
]
for f in findings:f['verification']=NAME
notes['findings'].extend(findings)
rows=[
 ('BERT model loading has obsolete stub prose around a real implementation','Loader/provider docs still describe unavailable weight injection.', ['crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/loader.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/provider.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/construct.rs'], 'Actual load_into_model_from_bytes validates, extracts and constructs weights. WeightLoadingNotImplemented remains only as an old enum/test surface. Orientation is already transposed by loaded.rs despite comments saying a future check must decide. These are stale implementation claims, not evidence of random-weight fallback in load.'),
 ('Tensor expectations and extraction repeat the same architecture recipe','expected_tensors is called canonical, while extract_all_tensors_from_bytes separately spells every name/shape.', ['crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/loader.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/loaded.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/safetensors_io.rs'], 'Validation and extraction share the small dtype/rank decoder only at the latter boundary; names/shapes remain two manual lists. Current Q/K/V and nonsquare FFN orientations agree. A loaded struct is also publicly constructible and from_loaded trusts it. Do not infer one admission owner merely from a canonical-table comment.'),
 ('BERT padding is generated but loses custody before computation','Tokenizer emits attention masks ready for model use.', ['crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/tokenizer.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/provider.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/model.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/attention.rs'], 'Q339 traces the discarded mask through shared sync/async forward. Fixing only pooling cannot remove pad contributions already mixed by attention. Mixed-length batch/alone reference equality is the missing acceptance gate; no numerical run was attempted in this source pass.'),
 ('Reference evidence is narrower than the BERT tier guarantee','Validation docs advertise always-on cheap reference checks and an empirical tolerance.', ['crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/validation.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/config.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/wgpu_parity.rs'], 'The real fixture comparison is ignored and requires an external model directory; continuous corpus validation is still an ignored panic stub. Active no-model tests cover unloaded refusal, random shape/finiteness or synthetic CPU/GPU parity, not current released-model numerical fidelity. The source explicitly marks some limitations but its Tier1 overview remains misleading.'),
 ('BERT backend tracing is an intrusive diagnostic corridor','Trace recomputes prefixes and awaits readback at each boundary.', ['crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/provider.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/embeddings.rs'], 'It preserves finite/bit/identity evidence and explicitly says barriers perturb execution. Shallow clones are not claimed as historical snapshots. Async embed shares the ordinary model body and differs in awaited readback; it is not a second semantic implementation.'),
 ('BERT config names math that is not admitted or dispatched','hidden_act is preserved while FFN always calls GELU.', ['crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/config.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/feed_forward.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/loader.rs'], 'Q346 bounds this to unsupported activation values. No evidence contradicts supported GELU presets from this source trace. Head count/divisibility, zero dimensions, epsilon and sequence lengths need a structured config admission story instead of relying entirely on tensor assertions.'),
 ('Portable dense and sparse indexes share the nearest owner','SparseIndex is VectorIndex<K,SparseVector> via IndexVector.', ['crates/intel/esp/src/embed/index.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/sparse.rs'], 'This is actual sharing, with separate representation-specific metric arithmetic. Seventy original portable core tests pass freshly. Public serde and accepted NaN reveal admission/order holes despite ordinary equality/parity controls: Q343/Q344. No second independent sparse index is invented here.'),
 ('Burn acceleration duplicates ordering and normalization policy','cosine_top_k recomputes the dense metric and top-k outside VectorIndex.', ['crates/intel/esp/src/embed/index_burn.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/index.rs'], 'Q340/Q341 show present tie/near-zero divergence, not just duplicate code. Burn sorts whole score rows despite partial-sort prose in the nearby CPU owner; its full QxN readback bound is honestly documented. Fixed crossover thresholds are exported empirical constants, not freshly measured universal device policy.'),
 ('Affinity has independent CPU and accelerator filter loops','AffinityScan and affinity_pairs_over_index repeat self filtering, symmetric dedup and clamping.', ['crates/intel/esp/src/embed/affinity.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/index_burn.rs'], 'Q345 executes the shared flawed self-in-head assumption in portable CPU code; accelerator retains it by source. AffinityScan snapshots entry vectors but explicitly requires the same unchanged live index for every step. That caller-owned generation contract is not enforced by a token or hash.'),
 ('Search reconstruction admits dimensions without space agreement','Facade constructors validate dimension while provider and supplied index each retain a metric.', ['crates/intel/esp/src/embed/search.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/provider.rs'], 'Q342 reproduces contradictory reads in both representations. Provider metric, index metric and vector-model identity are different facts; dimensions alone are not provenance. index_mut/sparse_index_mut also permit replacement; a repair must preserve the invariant beyond constructors.'),
 ('Provider output cardinality is an explicit implementation contract','embed promises exactly one fixed-dimension vector per input in order.', ['crates/intel/esp/src/embed/provider.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/search.rs','crates/intel/eidetic-search/examples/eidetic-recall.rs'], 'The earlier recall zip concern is now qualified: callers rely on this trait contract and no current conforming provider was shown to return a short batch. SemanticSearch zip still returns Ok for a broken provider, and single embed_one selects the last of an overlong result. Treat defensive admission as a follow-up, not an established live provider bug.'),
 ('Sparse capability selection is a property probe, not a second store copy','SemanticSearch probes embed_sparse(empty) and keeps entries only in one representation.', ['crates/intel/esp/src/embed/search.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/provider.rs'], 'The probe ignores whether Some contains Err; later embedding reports that error. With_sparse_index does not require a sparse-capable provider, so a dense-only provider can pair with sparse data but text search refuses. Nearest preembedded and scores are separate capabilities. These contracts require precise caller-facing naming, not claims that all constructors expose identical operation sets.'),
 ('Lexical vectors retain an independent tokenization policy','ESP splits all alphanumeric runs directly; Eidetic Search first uses Genet UAX29 words.', ['crates/intel/esp/src/embed/lexical.rs','crates/intel/eidetic-search/src/tokenize.rs'], 'The search module says its tokenizer is public so ESP can share it, but this provider does not. Tokyo is one alphanumeric run here versus per-ideograph segments in Search tests. Unigram/bigram hashes are version-sensitive space identity and need explicit profile custody before a compatibility-preserving unification.'),
 ('Lexical dense/sparse generation actually shares hashed pairs','One accumulated sorted feature list feeds dense and sparse normalization.', ['crates/intel/esp/src/embed/lexical.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/sparse.rs'], 'Original tests preserve golden unigram bins, canonical n-gram orders, correlated token signal and sparse equivalence. Signed FNV hashing is explicitly lexical rather than semantic. Dimensions beyond u32 bucket representation can truncate, an extreme-size contract follow-up; no large allocation or corpus performance run.'),
 ('Typed vector persistence preserves integrity but not provider provenance','Dense/sparse representations have separate schema IDs and delegate to Eidetic typed payloads.', ['crates/intel/esp/src/embed/persistence.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/index.rs'], 'Both payload schemas record dimensions/metric/entries but no model revision, pooling/prefix/ngram/tokenizer identity. Listings are schema-wide and recommend caller-selected newest. The recall example therefore cannot verify same-dimension model-space compatibility from this type alone. Hash verification is not internal vector validation (Q343). No live store or sharing run was performed.'),
 ('Hash-addressed index serialization has an unresolved canonical-order boundary','VectorIndex entries remain a HashMap under derived Serialize.', ['crates/intel/esp/src/embed/index.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/persistence.rs'], 'Do not infer semantic corpus identity from identical source records unless the actual Eidetic serializer canonicalizes maps. Existing persistence tests exercise a cloned map, not independently rebuilt equivalent corpora. Trace the already-reviewed typed serializer before turning this into a content-ID finding.'),
 ('Stub vectors are honestly labeled but retain stale roadmap claims','The default stub says it is not a similarity signal.', ['crates/intel/esp/src/embed/stub.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/mod.rs'], 'Lexical embedding is already shipped in the default build, although stub prose calls it a future lexical feature. Whole-string FNV/xorshift and per-token feature hashing are intentionally different semantics; their tiny hash/normalization loops are independently duplicated utility code, not equivalent providers.'),
 ('One meaning manifest is a specific consumer pin, not all model admission','MEANING_MODEL_MANIFEST embeds the exact e5-base-v2 revision/artifact metadata file.', ['crates/intel/esp/src/embed/mod.rs',aux,'crates/intel/esp/src/embed/bert/provider.rs'], 'The auxiliary JSON was read and hashed separately from source-inventory counts. Generic BERT constructors remain caller-directed and do not enforce this one model. The file records a past local artifact comparison; this audit did not rehash external weights, fetch the model card or claim current numerical parity.'),
 ('Decoder attention is separate math with shared ownership inside its own paths','Full prefill and incremental/chunked attention delegate to forward_cached_chunked.', ['crates/intel/esp/src/infer/decoder/attention.rs'], 'Split-half RoPE, GQA grouping and causal masking are explicit; tests perturb future tokens and compare chunk/full variants. These are stronger semantics than BERT shape-only checks but still self-comparisons without fresh execution here. Cache appends precede potential device-sync errors, so retry/rollback custody needs the remaining generation caller review.'),
 ('Inference import and qualification examples expose a controlled host lane','Examples store model/template/row and separately qualify an explicitly measured formatted candidate.', ['crates/intel/esp/examples/import_inference_model.rs','crates/intel/esp/examples/qualify_inference_model.rs','crates/intel/esp/Cargo.toml'], 'They bootstrap no download loop and delegate format/qualification policy to unread layer_library. They accept caller-supplied qualification JSON and label device by measured_loader prefix. Do not claim measurement authenticity or published-runtime admission until those owner sources are read; decoder/LoRA actor features stay opt-in.'),
 ('Portable embedding verification excludes model and GPU acceptance','Seven original source modules compile together in their unchanged embed namespace.', ['crates/intel/esp/src/embed/index.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/search.rs','crates/intel/esp/src/embed/sparse.rs'], '77 tests pass:70original plus7audit cases, comprising six public API observations and one exact crate-private comparator cycle. No copied algorithm or mock index/provider owner. Retained serde/json suppliers are hashed; no whole ESP infer/actor/Burn/model-session build or real corpus/GPU/store proof is claimed.'),
]
